<div class="research-body">

  <div class="project-feature">
    <div class="project-feature__label">Core Program</div>
    <h2>Comparative movement across desert ecosystems</h2>
    <p>A central strand of Erell Institute research tracks how lizards use space across a range of desert systems — from the Namib and Kalahari to the Chihuahuan and Gobi deserts. By applying consistent methods (step-length analysis, home-range estimation, path tortuosity) across species and sites, the program builds a comparative picture of how habitat, body size, phylogeny, and social context shape individual movement decisions.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="project-feature">
    <div class="project-feature__label">Current Focus</div>
    <h2>Drone-assisted habitat selection analysis</h2>
    <p>The 2025 Lizard Camp field season field-tested step-selection analysis using drone-acquired imagery to characterize fine-scale habitat structure at individual GPS fixes. This approach allows movement data and habitat data to be collected at the same spatial and temporal resolution — closing a long-standing methodological gap in lizard movement ecology.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="project-feature">
    <div class="project-feature__label">Previous Work</div>
    <h2>Intraspecific and demographic variation in movement</h2>
    <p>Multiple projects have documented how movement characteristics vary within a single species by sex, age class, and individual identity. Work on species including <em>Meroles anchietae</em>, <em>Phrynocephalus versicolor</em>, and <em>Aspidoscelis inornatus</em> reveals that intraspecific variation in movement can be as large as interspecific variation — with implications for how we interpret species-level movement summaries.</p>
  </div>

